  "body": {
    "id": "prop:fixed-alphabet-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\mathbf P=(P_n)\\in\\mathcal B_{\\epsilon,M,c_0,v_0,v_1}\\) with \\(d_n=d\\) fixed, the balanced-mass and nondegenerate-variance conditions remain in force, and \\(thm:local-linearization\\) and \\(thm:endpoint-equivalence\\) reduce to the classical efficient-influence-function central limit theorem, ratio-consistent variance estimation, and Wald interval for the ATE with fixed discrete covariates.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(\\mathbf P=(P_n)\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal B_{\\epsilon,M,c_0,v_0,v_1}\\)",
      "\\(\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(M\\)",
      "\\(c_0\\)",
      "\\(v_0\\)",
      "\\(v_1\\)",
      "\\(d_n\\)",
      "\\(d\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:balanced-cell-masses",
      "ass:efficient-variance-band",
      "def:balanced-local-class",
      "thm:local-linearization",
      "thm:endpoint-equivalence"
    ],
    "justification": "Fixed alphabet validates the one-step benchmark and makes all-heavy failure exponentially rare.",
    "gap": "The heavy-light studentized radius and Wald endpoint conclusion remain dependent on the missing hybrid score.",
    "consumer": "It is the sanity check against classical fixed-discrete-covariate ATE inference.",
    "proof_tex": "If \\(d_n=d\\) is fixed, then \\(d=o(\\sqrt n)\\), so the dimension condition is\nautomatic.  Membership in\n\\(\\mathcal B_{\\epsilon,M,c_0,v_0,v_1}\\) retains, by definition, the lower cell\nmass bound \\(P_n(X=k)\\ge c_0/d\\) and the variance band\n\\(v_0M^2\\le V_{\\mathrm{eff}}(P_n)\\le v_1M^2\\).  Substituting fixed \\(d\\) into\n\\(thm:local\\text{-}linearization\\) gives the efficient-influence-function\nexpansion, its standardized normal limit, and ratio consistency of both stated\nvariance estimators.  Substituting the same facts into\n\\(thm:endpoint\\text{-}equivalence\\) identifies the first-order endpoints with\nthose of the saturated-cell Wald interval.  These are exactly the three\nconclusions asserted in the proposition.",
